00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.050
First off, rewrite is
not a viable option

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:02.905
for many workloads.

00:02:02.905 --> 00:02:05.530
Yes, you might want to take and
rewrite all these applications.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.660
But think about a company that
has hundreds and thousands

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.800
of legacy applications.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.780
It becomes really,
really complicated.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.940
Sometimes, these
applications are so old,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.960
they don't even have
access to the source code

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.418
or the developers who actually
wrote those applications are

00:02:17.418 --> 00:02:18.690
no longer here.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:19.760
Second thing is, yeah.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.378
The question I get
asked is, well,

00:02:21.378 --> 00:02:23.045
why can't we just
lift and shift to VMs?

00:02:23.045 --> 00:02:24.750
Sure, it's a safe bet.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.780
But there are a lot
of those benefits

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.760
from the new
technologies [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.070
And there's also a
significant cost increase

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.602
due to lack of
density per host VMs.

00:02:33.602 --> 00:02:35.810
And then finally, the thing
that I tell the customers

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.600
is that most importantly, we
don't see app organization

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.100
as a binary problem.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.690
Even if you can get--

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.948
if you can't get all the
benefits, customers are like,

00:02:43.948 --> 00:02:45.240
can I get some of the benefits?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.240
And that's what I talk
about when we say meeting

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.620
the customers where they are.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.240
Depending on your
risk of portfolio,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.950
depending on the
application portfolio,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.230
we have choices to take them
anywhere that they want.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.320
Which brings us to what
is Google's point of view

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.080
when it comes to modernization.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.950
It basically is the
future of applications

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.800
and the infrastructure
that they run on

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.260
is created with containerized
micro services, managed

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.520
to a declarative system.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.950
And declarative
system is where code

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.140
represents the final state.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.180
So Kubernetes is
a perfect example.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.050
With the single
control experience

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.750
that uses a service mesh to span
all the applicable locations.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.950
So service mesh, as you know,
is a configurable low latency

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.970
infrastructure that
ensures communication

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.470
among these continuous
applications.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.050
So, now that you have
these applications running

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:30.670
in these containers, you want
to be ensuring that there's

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.360
mutual [INAUDIBLE] you get
all those components right out

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.620
of the box using service mesh
to span all applicable locations

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:37.776
[INAUDIBLE].

00:03:37.776 --> 00:03:40.410
And how do we take
all of that we

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.050
want to achieve at modernization
and put it into a solution,

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.820
just call it Anthos.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.760
So Anthos is a managed
application platform

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.430
that's based on Kubernetes,
Istio, and Knative

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.012
for enterprises that want
faster modernization, right?

00:03:53.012 --> 00:03:54.470
And we talk about
this work up when

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:57.180
I say, tools to perform
no touch migration.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.600
And greater consistency in a
hybrid and multi cloud world.

