clusters, whether it's

00:10:00.843 --> 00:10:03.640
on prem on the
cloud, this allows

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:05.950
you to make sure you do that.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.700
Because it's also based off Git.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.988
So it enables auditability and
review of CI policy as scope.

00:10:10.988 --> 00:10:13.030
So let's put all of these
things together, right?

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.110
We talked about service mesh.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.777
We talked about
configuration management

00:10:15.777 --> 00:10:18.040
and running these
applications [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.720
So out of the box,
you can take this

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:20.928
and that's what Anthos comes.

00:10:20.928 --> 00:10:23.512
With Migrate for Anthos, we're
able to move these applications

00:10:23.512 --> 00:10:25.740
and run them and manage
these independent of where

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.850
you are running it
below, whether it's

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:32.030
going to be on Google Cloud,
on-prem hypervisor, AWS,

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.030
whatever it is.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.360
So, how does it work?

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.720
Very quickly, we
identify the workloads,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.000
whether it's running
on VMs or on-prem.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.650
And then Migrate for
Anthos, once we've

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.600
identified the
workloads, we take them

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.130
and we auto containerize them.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:50.550
And now you're actually
generating the CI/CD artifacts

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:51.230
that you need.

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:52.980
So basically, Migrate
for Anthos basically

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:55.680
generates a Kubernetes YAML
file in a Docker image,

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.440
and a Docker file, which you
can then take and actually

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.870
move them into Anthos,
or connect them

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.007
to a CI/CD pipeline.

00:11:02.007 --> 00:11:04.590
And now once you're running it
on Anthos, like I talked about,

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:07.320
you can use all the service mesh
and logging and capabilities

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:09.620
as well.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:12.940
The key two important
things over here to remember

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:14.300
is it's not a lift and shift.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.700
You're not completely
taking everything and simply

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.178
just moving it into
a Docker container.

00:11:19.178 --> 00:11:20.470
You can see the things in red--

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.460
Kernel drivers,
Management Agents, Logs--

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:23.970
things that do not matter.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:27.530
You're not actually taking
them and moving them

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:29.180
into the container
world as well.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.340
And another key
important thing, which

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.650
is that it also handles
persistent workloads.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:36.150
So as most of you
know, for example,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.192
if you're running say your
application on VM ware

00:11:38.192 --> 00:11:41.292
and talking to a [INAUDIBLE],,
once you've migrated it

00:11:41.292 --> 00:11:43.750
to Anthos, it actually converts
them to persistent volumes.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:46.125
So right out of the box, you're
already in the Kubernetes

00:11:46.125 --> 00:11:47.920
ecosystem and how
to be able to use it

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:49.712
so it becomes easy for
you to run on Anthos

00:11:49.712 --> 00:11:52.310
and get all the benefits like
RBAC, auto-scaling, everything

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:53.827
right out of the box.

00:11:53.827 --> 00:11:55.910
So now that I've done the
