of these micro services,

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:10.210
we get additional information,
like queries per second

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:11.050
and so on.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:13.710
We also get health status
of each of these services

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:14.910
right on the Topology page.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.750
The recommendation
service is healthy.

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:18.360
But checkout service is not.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.070
So if we go to the
checkout service,

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:23.560
we can delve deeper into
an individual service.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:25.450
So now I'm in the
checkout service.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:28.050
And we can see what's
going on with this service.

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.660
So up top, we have a
timeline of the health

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:32.100
status of that service.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.110
And right now, it's set
to 1 hour time span.

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:35.818
And you can see that
it's definitely been

00:21:35.818 --> 00:21:37.275
unhealthy for the last hour.

00:21:37.275 --> 00:21:38.400
Let's check it for one day.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:41.710
I want to see when did it
start becoming unhealthy.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:45.220
So you can see that
yesterday around 3:30 PM

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:47.320
is when it started
becoming unhealthy.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:49.600
And what does that mean
that it was unhealthy?

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:51.730
Well we've created some
service level objectives

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.170
for this particular service.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:54.940
So here's the SLO.

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:56.710
If I go into the
SLO details page,

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:58.750
I've created a single SLO.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.010
It's a latency based SLO.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:03.292
You can create one or
more SLOs per service.

00:22:03.292 --> 00:22:04.750
Let's edit this
SLO and let's start

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:06.370
at the beginning on
what that even means.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:07.787
So the first thing
I want to do is

00:22:07.787 --> 00:22:10.360
I want to select my
service level indicator.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.640
It could either be availability
based, latency based,

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:14.090
or even a custom metrics base.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:17.260
In this case, we're doing
a latency based indicator.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:19.700
And it's a request based SLO.

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.010
We go to the next screen.

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.260
I've set my latency
threshold to two seconds

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.110
for the 95th percentile latency.

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:28.870
So I'd like my 95th
percentile latency

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:30.910
to be two seconds or lower.

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:33.100
And you can see from
the historical data

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:36.220
that it's been creeping up
over time, all the way up to 8,

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:37.720
9, 10 seconds.

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.460
No wonder it's acting unhealthy.

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:42.950
Here I can set the compliance
period for a single day,

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:43.790
for example.

00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:48.110
I'd like 75% of the time to
be within that service level

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:51.590
objective, which is that I would
like my 95th percentile to be

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:54.290
two seconds or less,
75% of the time.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:56.460
Obviously we are
not meeting that.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:58.130
And that's why it's unhealthy.

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:00.140
You can also sets
alerts that gets

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:04.040
fired as it becomes unhealthy.

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:06.410
We can also get into
further metrics.

00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:10.050
Which particular
deployment is causing that?

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:13.200
So in this case, I can
break this down by location.

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:15.200
It could be a single
region or a single cluster

00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:17.970
or a specific instance
of that cluster.

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:20.570
I can go very granular
into figuring out

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:22.267
exactly what's
causing that problem

00:23:22.267 --> 00:23:23.850
and then go and
mitigate that problem.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.100
In this case, we're just
running it into a single prod

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:27.013
cluster in one pod.

00:23:27.013 --> 00:23:28.430
But let's say this
application was

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:30.840
running on multiple clusters
in multiple regions.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:35.040
You can start to now drill
this down and troubleshoot it.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:38.370
Speaking of security postures,
if I go to Connected Services,

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:41.280
for the checkout service, I
can see both requests coming

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:42.795
in and out of that service.

00:23:42.795 --> 00:23:44.850
So I can see who is
talking to that service

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.067
and who does that
service talk to.

00:23:47.067 --> 00:23:48.525
So I'm in the
inbound request page,

00:23:48.525 --> 00:23:50.460
and I can see that I'm
only receiving requests

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:54.570
from the front end service.

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.497
And I can also see
the security posture,

00:23:56.497 --> 00:23:58.830
which is this green lock,
representing that this traffic

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.882
is encrypted using MTLS.

00:24:00.882 --> 00:24:02.340
Similarly, I can
go to the outbound

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:05.520
and you can see a ton of other
services that it talks to.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.350
And every single one
of these services, I

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:09.990
can see the port as well
as the security posture

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:12.302
where it's all encrypted.

00:24:12.302 --> 00:24:13.760
So hopefully, this
gave you an idea

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:16.630
of how we can take a
monolithic virtual machine,

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:18.860
use Migrate for Anthos to
convert it into a container

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.960
and run it onto Kubernetes.

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:23.420
And then over time, as we
refactor this application

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:26.930
into micro services, we
can use Anthos Service Mesh

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:28.790
to implement some
of the Google SRE

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:32.040
best practices
into your platform.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:32.540
All right.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:34.170
So let's recap real quick.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:36.290
So the first thing
we identified the VM,

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:39.240
running in virtual machine,
that's on the left hand side.

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:40.490
We took a WebLogic server.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:43.580
The first thing we want to
do is migrated to Anthos.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:46.238
So uplift the platform.

00:24:46.238 --> 00:24:47.780
We did that using
Migrate for Anthos.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:50.330
We made the VM much more
lightweight by excluding

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:53.390
some of those files and
folders that Harish talked

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:55.110
about before.

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.980
And then we deployed that to a
staging cluster inside of GKE.

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.900
Now we can start using
benefits of ASM and such.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:03.800
Now sometime later,
over time, we've

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.430
termed that service into a
number of micro services.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:09.020
And at that point,
we can really start

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:11.300
to get the value of
Anthos Service Mesh.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:12.860
We can look at the topology.

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:13.890
We can get the metrics.

00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:16.108
We can start setting
SLOs per service.

00:25:16.108 --> 00:25:17.900
And we can really start
to incorporate some

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:19.562
of those SRE best practices.

00:25:19.562 --> 00:25:21.020
So hopefully, that
gave you an idea

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:22.850
of a lifecycle of
an application going

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:25.310
from a virtual
machine inside VMware

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:28.130
all the way to micro
services inside of Anthos.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:29.888
Back to you Harish.

00:25:29.888 --> 00:25:31.180
HARISH JAYAKUMAR: Thanks Ameer.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:33.250
I know we covered a
lot of stuff today.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.820
So as next steps, I'm going to
leave you with two links which

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:37.560
is going to help you.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:42.320
First is bit_ly/java-anthos,
where you can learn more about

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:45.920
how you can use Anthos for your
Java modernization journey.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:51.292
And then link two is
